.sp
See the
\f(CRpcre2_set_optimize\fP
documentation for details on these optimizations.
.SS "Setting compilation controls"
.sp
The following modifiers affect the compilation process or request information
about the pattern. There are single\-letter abbreviations for some that are
heavily used in the test files.
.sp
.ti +2
/B  bincode                   show binary code without lengths
.br
.ti +2
    bsr=[anycrlf|unicode]     specify \(rsR handling
.br
.ti +2
    callout_info              show callout information
.br
.ti +2
    convert=<options>         request foreign pattern conversion
.br
.ti +2
    convert_glob_escape=c     set glob escape character
.br
.ti +2
    convert_glob_separator=c  set glob separator character
.br
.ti +2
    convert_length            set convert buffer length
.br
.ti +2
    debug                     same as info,fullbincode
.br
.ti +2
    expand                    expand repetition syntax in pattern
.br
.ti +2
    framesize                 show matching frame size
.br
.ti +2
    fullbincode               show binary code with lengths
.br
.ti +2
/I  info                      show info about compiled pattern
.br
.ti +2
    hex                       unquoted characters are hexadecimal
.br
.ti +2
    jit[=<number>]            use JIT
.br
.ti +2
    jitfast                   use JIT fast path
.br
.ti +2
    jitverify                 verify JIT use
.br
.ti +2
    locale=<name>             use this locale
.br
.ti +2
    max_pattern_compiled      ) set maximum compiled pattern
.br
.ti +2
               _length=<n>    )   length (bytes)
.br
.ti +2
    max_pattern_length=<n>    set maximum pattern length (code units)
.br
.ti +2
    max_varlookbehind=<n>     set maximum variable lookbehind length
.br
.ti +2
    memory                    show memory used
.br
.ti +2
    newline=<type>            set newline type
.br
.ti +2
    null_context              compile with a NULL context
.br
.ti +2
    null_pattern              pass pattern as NULL
.br
.ti +2
    parens_nest_limit=<n>     set maximum parentheses depth
.br
.ti +2
    posix                     use the POSIX API
.br
.ti +2
    posix_nosub               use the POSIX API with REG_NOSUB
.br
.ti +2
    push                      push compiled pattern onto the stack
.br
.ti +2
    pushcopy                  push a copy onto the stack
.br
.ti +2
    pushtablescopy            push a copy with tables onto the stack
.br
.ti +2
    stackguard=<number>       test the stackguard feature
.br
.ti +2
    subject_literal           treat all subject lines as literal
.br
.ti +2
    tables=[0|1|2|3]          select internal tables
.br
.ti +2
    use_length                do not zero\-terminate the pattern
.br
.ti +2
    utf8_input                treat input as UTF\-8
.sp
The effects of these modifiers are described in the following sections.
.SS "Newline and \(rsR handling"
.sp
The \f(CRbsr\fP modifier specifies what \f(CR\(rsR\fP in a pattern should match. If it is
set to "anycrlf", \f(CR\(rsR\fP matches CR, LF, or CRLF only. If it is set to "unicode",
\f(CR\(rsR\fP matches any Unicode newline sequence. The default can be specified when
PCRE2 is built; if it is not, the default is set to Unicode.
.sp
The \f(CRnewline\fP modifier specifies which characters are to be interpreted as
newlines, both in the pattern and in subject lines. The type must be one of CR,
LF, CRLF, ANYCRLF, ANY, or NUL (in upper or lower case).
.SS "Information about a pattern"
.sp
The \f(CRdebug\fP modifier is a shorthand for \f(CRinfo,fullbincode\fP, requesting
all available information.
.sp
The \f(CRbincode\fP modifier causes a representation of the compiled code to be
output after compilation. This information does not contain length and offset
values, which ensures that the same output is generated for different internal
link sizes and different code unit widths. By using \f(CRbincode\fP, the same
regression tests can be used in different environments.
.sp
The \f(CRfullbincode\fP modifier, by contrast, \fIdoes\fP include length and
offset values. This is used in a few special tests that run only for specific
code unit widths and link sizes, and is also useful for one\-off tests.
.sp
The \f(CRinfo\fP modifier requests information about the compiled pattern
(whether it is anchored, has a fixed first character, and so on). The
information is obtained from the pcre2_pattern_info() function. Here are
some typical examples:
.sp
.ti +2
  re> /(?i)(^a|^b)/m,info
.br
.ti +2
Capture group count = 1
.br
.ti +2
Compile options: multiline
.br
.ti +2
Overall options: caseless multiline
.br
.ti +2
First code unit at start or follows newline
.br
.ti +2
Subject length lower bound = 1
.sp
.ti +2
  re> /(?i)abc/info
.br
.ti +2
Capture group count = 0
.br
.ti +2
Compile options: <none>
.br
.ti +2
Overall options: caseless
.br
.ti +2
First code unit = \*(Aqa\*(Aq (caseless)
.br
.ti +2
Last code unit = \*(Aqc\*(Aq (caseless)
.br
.ti +2
Subject length lower bound = 3
.sp
"Compile options" are those specified by modifiers; "overall options" have
added options that are taken or deduced from the pattern. If both sets of
options are the same, just a single "options" line is output; if there are no
options, the line is omitted. "First code unit" is where any match must start;
if there is more than one they are listed as "starting code units". "Last code
unit" is the last literal code unit that must be present in any match. This is
not necessarily the last character. These lines are omitted if no starting or
ending code units are recorded. The subject length line is omitted when
\f(CRno_start_optimize\fP is set because the minimum length is not calculated
when it can never be used.
.sp
The \f(CRframesize\fP modifier shows the size, in bytes, of each storage frame
used by pcre2_match() for handling backtracking. The size depends on the
number of capturing parentheses in the pattern. A vector of these frames is
used at matching time; its overall size is shown when the \f(CRheapframes_size\fP
subject modifier is set.
.sp
The \f(CRcallout_info\fP modifier requests information about all the callouts in
the pattern. A list of them is output at the end of any other information that
is requested. For each callout, either its number or string is given, followed
by the item that follows it in the pattern.
.SS "Passing a null context"
.sp
Normally, \f(CRpcre2test\fP passes a context block to pcre2_compile(). If
the \f(CRnull_context\fP modifier is set, however, NULL is passed. This is for
testing that pcre2_compile() behaves correctly in this case (it uses
default values).
.SS "Passing a null pattern"
.sp
The \f(CRnull_pattern\fP modifier is for testing the behaviour of
pcre2_compile() when the pattern argument is NULL. The length value
passed is the default \fBPCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED\fP unless \f(CRuse_length\fP is set.
Any length other than zero causes an error.
.SS "Specifying pattern characters in hexadecimal"
.sp
The \f(CRhex\fP modifier specifies that the characters of the pattern, except for
substrings enclosed in single or double quotes, are to be interpreted as pairs
of hexadecimal digits. This feature is provided as a way of creating patterns
that contain binary zeros and other non\-printing characters. White space is
permitted between pairs of digits. For example, this pattern contains three
characters:
.sp
.ti +2
/ab 32 59/hex
.sp
Parts of such a pattern are taken literally if quoted. This pattern contains
nine characters, only two of which are specified in hexadecimal:
.sp
.ti +2
/ab "literal" 32/hex
.sp
Either single or double quotes may be used. There is no way of including
the delimiter within a substring. The \f(CRhex\fP and \f(CRexpand\fP modifiers are
mutually exclusive.
.SS "Specifying the pattern\(cqs length"
.sp
By default, patterns are passed to the compiling functions as zero\-terminated
strings but can be passed by length instead of being zero\-terminated. The
\f(CRuse_length\fP modifier causes this to happen. Using a length happens
automatically (whether or not \f(CRuse_length\fP is set) when \f(CRhex\fP is set,
because patterns specified in hexadecimal may contain binary zeros.
.sp
If \f(CRhex\fP or \f(CRuse_length\fP is used with the POSIX wrapper API (see
"Using the POSIX wrapper API"
below), the REG_PEND extension is used to pass the pattern\(cqs length.
.SS "Specifying a maximum for variable lookbehinds"
.sp
Variable lookbehind assertions are supported only if, for each one, there is a
maximum length (in characters) that it can match. There is a limit on this,
whose default can be set at build time, with an ultimate default of 255. The
\f(CRmax_varlookbehind\fP modifier uses the pcre2_set_max_varlookbehind()
function to change the limit. Lookbehinds whose branches each match a fixed
length are limited to 65535 characters per branch.
.SS "Specifying wide characters in 16\-bit and 32\-bit modes"
.sp
In 16\-bit and 32\-bit modes, all input is automatically treated as UTF\-8 and
translated to UTF\-16 or UTF\-32 when the \f(CRutf\fP modifier is set. For testing
the 16\-bit and 32\-bit libraries in non\-UTF mode, the \f(CRutf8_input\fP modifier
can be used. It is mutually exclusive with \f(CRutf\fP. Input lines are
interpreted as UTF\-8 as a means of specifying wide characters. More details are
given in
"Input encoding"
above.
.SS "Generating long repetitive patterns"
.sp
Some tests use long patterns that are very repetitive. Instead of creating a
very long input line for such a pattern, you can use a special repetition
feature, similar to the one described for subject lines above. If the
\f(CRexpand\fP modifier is present on a pattern, parts of the pattern that have
the form
.sp
.ti +2
\(rs[<characters>]{<count>}
.sp
are expanded before the pattern is passed to pcre2_compile(). For
example, \(rs[AB]{6000} is expanded to "ABAB..." 6000 times. This construction
cannot be nested. An initial "\(rs[" sequence is recognized only if "]{" followed
by decimal digits and "}" is found later in the pattern. If not, the characters
remain in the pattern unaltered. The \f(CRexpand\fP and \f(CRhex\fP modifiers are
mutually exclusive.
.sp
If part of an expanded pattern looks like an expansion, but is really part of
the actual pattern, unwanted expansion can be avoided by giving two values in
the quantifier. For example, \(rs[AB]{6000,6000} is not recognized as an
expansion item.
.sp
If the \f(CRinfo\fP modifier is set on an expanded pattern, the result of the
expansion is included in the information that is output.
.SS "JIT compilation"
.sp
Just\-in\-time (JIT) compiling is a heavyweight optimization that can greatly
speed up pattern matching. See the
\f(CRpcre2jit\fP
documentation for details. JIT compiling happens, optionally, after a pattern
has been successfully compiled into an internal form. The JIT compiler converts
this to optimized machine code. It needs to know whether the match\-time options
\fBPCRE2_PARTIAL_HARD\fP and \fBPCRE2_PARTIAL_SOFT\fP are going to be used, because
different code is generated for the different cases. See the \f(CRpartial\fP
modifier in "Subject Modifiers"
below
for details of how these options are specified for each match attempt.
.sp
JIT compilation is requested by the \f(CRjit\fP pattern modifier, which may
optionally be followed by an equals sign and a number in the range 0 to 7.
The three bits that make up the number specify which of the three JIT operating
modes are to be compiled:
.sp
.ti +2
1  compile JIT code for non\-partial matching
.br
.ti +2
2  compile JIT code for soft partial matching
.br
.ti +2
4  compile JIT code for hard partial matching
.sp
The possible values for the \f(CRjit\fP modifier are therefore:
.sp
.ti +2
0  disable JIT
.br
.ti +2
1  normal matching only
.br
.ti +2
2  soft partial matching only
.br
.ti +2
3  normal and soft partial matching
.br
.ti +2
4  hard partial matching only
.br
.ti +2
6  soft and hard partial matching only
.br
.ti +2
7  all three modes
.sp
If no number is given, 7 is assumed. The phrase "partial matching" means a call
to pcre2_match() with either the \fBPCRE2_PARTIAL_SOFT\fP or the
\fBPCRE2_PARTIAL_HARD\fP option set. Note that such a call may return a complete
match; the options enable the possibility of a partial match, but do not
require it. Note also that if you request JIT compilation only for partial
matching (for example, jit=2) but do not set the \f(CRpartial\fP modifier on a
subject line, that match will not use JIT code because none was compiled for
non\-partial matching.
.sp
If JIT compilation is successful, the compiled JIT code will automatically be
used when an appropriate type of match is run, except when incompatible
run\-time options are specified. For more details, see the
\f(CRpcre2jit\fP
documentation. See also the \f(CRjitstack\fP modifier below for a way of
setting the size of the JIT stack.
.sp
If the \f(CRjitfast\fP modifier is specified, matching is done using the JIT
"fast path" interface, pcre2_jit_match(), which skips some of the sanity
checks that are done by pcre2_match(), and of course does not work when
JIT is not supported. If \f(CRjitfast\fP is specified without \f(CRjit\fP, jit=7 is
assumed.
.sp
If the \f(CRjitverify\fP modifier is specified, information about the compiled
pattern shows whether JIT compilation was or was not successful. If
\f(CRjitverify\fP is specified without \f(CRjit\fP, jit=7 is assumed. If JIT
compilation is successful when \f(CRjitverify\fP is set, the text "(JIT)" is
added to the first output line after a match or non match when JIT\-compiled
code was actually used in the match.
.SS "Setting a locale"
.sp
The \f(CRlocale\fP modifier must specify the name of a locale, for example:
.sp
.ti +2
/pattern/locale=fr_FR
.sp
The given locale is set, pcre2_maketables() is called to build a set of
character tables for the locale, and this is then passed to
pcre2_compile() when compiling the regular expression. The same tables
are used when matching the following subject lines. The \f(CRlocale\fP modifier
applies only to the pattern on which it appears, but can be given in a
\f(CR#pattern\fP command if a default is needed. Setting a locale and alternate
character tables are mutually exclusive.
.SS "Showing pattern memory"
.sp
The \f(CRmemory\fP modifier causes the size in bytes of the memory used to hold
the compiled pattern to be output. This does not include the size of the
\fBpcre2_code\fP block; it is just the actual compiled data. If the pattern is
subsequently passed to the JIT compiler, the size of the JIT compiled code is
also output. Here is an example:
.sp
.ti +2
  re> /a(b)c/jit,memory
.br
.ti +2
Memory allocation (code space): 21
.br
.ti +2
Memory allocation (JIT code): 1910
.SS "Limiting nested parentheses"
.sp
The \f(CRparens_nest_limit\fP modifier sets a limit on the depth of nested
parentheses in a pattern. Breaching the limit causes a compilation error.
The default for the library is set when PCRE2 is built, but \f(CRpcre2test\fP
sets its own default of 220, which is required for running the standard test
suite.
.SS "Limiting the pattern length"
.sp
The \f(CRmax_pattern_length\fP modifier sets a limit, in code units, to the
length of pattern that pcre2_compile() will accept. Breaching the limit
causes a compilation error. The default is the largest number a \fBPCRE2_SIZE\fP
variable can hold (essentially unlimited).
.SS "Limiting the size of a compiled pattern"
.sp
The \f(CRmax_pattern_compiled_length\fP modifier sets a limit, in bytes, to the
amount of memory used by a compiled pattern. Breaching the limit causes a
compilation error. The default is the largest number a \fBPCRE2_SIZE\fP variable can
hold (essentially unlimited).
.SS "Using the POSIX wrapper API"
.sp
The \f(CRposix\fP and \f(CRposix_nosub\fP modifiers cause \f(CRpcre2test\fP to call
PCRE2 via the POSIX wrapper API rather than its native API. When
\f(CRposix_nosub\fP is used, the POSIX option \f(CRREG_NOSUB\fP is passed to
\f(CRregcomp()\fP. The POSIX wrapper supports only the 8\-bit library. Note that
it does not imply POSIX matching semantics; for more detail see the
\f(CRpcre2posix\fP
documentation. The following pattern modifiers set options for the
\f(CRregcomp()\fP function:
.sp
.ti +2
caseless           REG_ICASE
.br
.ti +2
multiline          REG_NEWLINE
.br
.ti +2
dotall             REG_DOTALL     )
.br
.ti +2
ungreedy           REG_UNGREEDY   ) These options are not part of
.br
.ti +2
ucp                REG_UCP        )   the POSIX standard
.br
.ti +2
utf                REG_UTF8       )
.sp
The \f(CRregerror_buffsize\fP modifier specifies a size for the error buffer that
is passed to \f(CRregerror()\fP in the event of a compilation error. For example:
.sp
.ti +2
/abc/posix,regerror_buffsize=20
.sp
This provides a means of testing the behaviour of \f(CRregerror()\fP when the
buffer is too small for the error message. If this modifier has not been set, a
large buffer is used.
.sp
The \f(CRaftertext\fP and \f(CRallaftertext\fP subject modifiers work as described
below. All other modifiers are either ignored, with a warning message, or cause
an error.
.sp
The pattern is passed to \f(CRregcomp()\fP as a zero\-terminated string by
default, but if the \f(CRuse_length\fP or \f(CRhex\fP modifiers are set, the
const:REG_PEND extension is used to pass it by length.
.SS "Testing the stack guard feature"
.sp
The \f(CRstackguard\fP modifier is used to test the use of
pcre2_set_compile_recursion_guard(), a function that is provided to
enable stack availability to be checked during compilation (see the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
documentation for details). If the number specified by the modifier is greater
than zero, pcre2_set_compile_recursion_guard() is called to set up
callback from pcre2_compile() to a local function. The argument it
receives is the current nesting parenthesis depth; if this is greater than the
value given by the modifier, non\-zero is returned, causing the compilation to
be aborted.
.SS "Using alternative character tables"
.sp
The value specified for the \f(CRtables\fP modifier must be one of the digits 0,
1, 2, or 3. It causes a specific set of built\-in character tables to be passed
to pcre2_compile(). This is used in the PCRE2 tests to check behaviour
with different character tables. The digit specifies the tables as follows:
.sp
.ti +2
0   do not pass any special character tables
.br
.ti +2
1   the default ASCII tables, as distributed in
.br
.ti +2
      pcre2_chartables.c.dist
.br
.ti +2
2   a set of tables defining ISO 8859 characters
.br
.ti +2
3   a set of tables loaded by the #loadtables command
.sp
In tables 2, some characters whose codes are greater than 128 are identified as
letters, digits, spaces, etc. Tables 3 can be used only after a
\f(CR#loadtables\fP command has loaded them from a binary file. Setting alternate
character tables and a locale are mutually exclusive.
.SS "Setting certain match controls"
.sp
The following modifiers are really subject modifiers, and are described under
"Subject Modifiers" below. However, they may be included in a pattern\(cqs
modifier list, in which case they are applied to every subject line that is
processed with that pattern. These modifiers do not affect the compilation
process.
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
