.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
These modifiers may not appear in a \f(CR#pattern\fP command. If you want them as
defaults, set them in a \f(CR#subject\fP command.
.SS "Specifying literal subject lines"
.sp
If the \f(CRsubject_literal\fP modifier is present on a pattern, all the subject
lines that it matches are taken as literal strings, with no interpretation of
backslashes. It is not possible to set subject modifiers on such lines, but any
that are set as defaults by a \f(CR#subject\fP command are recognized.
.SS "Saving a compiled pattern"
.sp
When a pattern with the \f(CRpush\fP modifier is successfully compiled, it is
pushed onto a stack of compiled patterns, and \f(CRpcre2test\fP expects the next
line to contain a new pattern (or a command) instead of a subject line. This
facility is used when saving compiled patterns to a file, as described in the
section entitled "Saving and restoring compiled patterns"
below.
If \f(CRpushcopy\fP is used instead of \f(CRpush\fP, a copy of the compiled
pattern is stacked, leaving the original as current, ready to match the
following input lines. This provides a way of testing the
pcre2_code_copy() function.
The \f(CRpush\fP and \f(CRpushcopy\fP  modifiers are incompatible with compilation
modifiers such as \f(CRglobal\fP that act at match time. Any that are specified
are ignored (for the stacked copy), with a warning message, except for
\f(CRreplace\fP, which causes an error. Note that \f(CRjitverify\fP, which is
allowed, does not carry through to any subsequent matching that uses a stacked
pattern.
.SS "Testing foreign pattern conversion"
.sp
The experimental foreign pattern conversion functions in PCRE2 can be tested by
setting the \f(CRconvert\fP modifier. Its argument is a colon\-separated list of
options, which set the equivalent option for the pcre2_pattern_convert()
function:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 23
\f(CRglob\fP
.ad l
\fBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB\fP
.TP 23
\f(CRglob_no_starstar\fP
.ad l
\fBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_STARSTAR\fP
.TP 23
\f(CRglob_no_wild_separator\fP
.ad l
\fBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_WILD_SEPARATOR\fP
.TP 23
\f(CRposix_basic\fP
.ad l
\fBPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_BASIC\fP
.TP 23
\f(CRposix_extended\fP
.ad l
\fBPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_EXTENDED\fP
.TP 23
\f(CRunset\fP
.ad l
Unset all options
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The "unset" value is useful for turning off a default that has been set by a
\f(CR#pattern\fP command. When one of these options is set, the input pattern is
passed to pcre2_pattern_convert(). If the conversion is successful, the
result is reflected in the output and then passed to pcre2_compile(). The
normal \f(CRutf\fP and \f(CRno_utf_check\fP options, if set, cause the
\fBPCRE2_CONVERT_UTF\fP and \fBPCRE2_CONVERT_NO_UTF_CHECK\fP options to be passed to
pcre2_pattern_convert().
.sp
By default, the conversion function is allowed to allocate a buffer for its
output. However, if the \f(CRconvert_length\fP modifier is set to a value greater
than zero, \f(CRpcre2test\fP passes a buffer of the given length. This makes it
possible to test the length check.
.sp
The \f(CRconvert_glob_escape\fP and \f(CRconvert_glob_separator\fP modifiers can be
used to specify the escape and separator characters for glob processing,
overriding the defaults, which are operating\-system dependent.
.SH "SUBJECT MODIFIERS"
.sp
The modifiers that can appear in subject lines and the \f(CR#subject\fP
command are of two types.
.SS "Setting match options"
.sp
The following modifiers set options for pcre2_match() or
pcre2_dfa_match(). See
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
for a description of their effects.
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
